SMERC Public Review License v1.0

Copyright (c) 2026 SMERC Macro Language Model contributors

This license applies to the SMERC Runtime Permission Layer repository and
associated source code, examples, documentation, schemas, reports, SDKs,
integrations, and reference materials, except where a file expressly states a
different license for third-party material.

1. Purpose

SMERC is made publicly available so security, platform, AI governance,
research, and prospective design-partner reviewers can inspect, run, evaluate,
and comment on the project before production use.

2. Permitted Public Review Use

Subject to this license, you may:

- view, clone, and copy the repository;
- run the reference implementation locally for non-production review;
- use the examples, tests, schemas, and reports to evaluate SMERC;
- modify the materials internally for non-production testing;
- create non-production pilot reports using metadata-only action examples; and
- share feedback, issues, pull requests, benchmark observations, and review
  comments with the project owner.

3. Commercial Use Requires Separate Permission

The permissions above do not include production use or commercial exploitation.
You must obtain a separate written commercial license or written agreement from
the project owner before you:

- deploy SMERC in a production, customer-facing, regulated, revenue-generating,
  or enterprise operational environment;
- embed SMERC, SMERC-derived logic, SMERC scoring methods, SMERC posture
  routing, SMERC schemas, SMERC SDKs, SMERC reports, or SMERC integrations into
  a commercial product, hosted service, internal enterprise platform, agent
  framework, cloud service, governance platform, security product, financial
  workflow, or managed service;
- offer SMERC or substantially similar SMERC-derived functionality as a paid,
  bundled, hosted, managed, resale, consulting, or integration service;
- sublicense, resell, rent, lease, or commercially distribute SMERC or a
  derivative work; or
- use SMERC to provide production decisioning, production enforcement,
  production audit evidence, production compliance support, or production
  safety claims for any third party.

4. Evaluation And Pilot Boundary

Non-production pilots are allowed only for evaluation and learning. They must
not execute production actions, control live customer assets, process secrets,
process regulated payloads, or create production compliance or safety claims
unless a separate written agreement permits that use.

